<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Niall of the Nine Hostages</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th century (traditional)</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Niall Noígíallach</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Niall of the Nine Hostages was a legendary Irish king of Tara, said to have reigned in the late 4th or early 5th century. He was the ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a> dynasties, whose northern branches founded Ailech, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-donegal">Tír Chonaill</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tir-eoghain">Tír Eoghain</a> and whose southern branches ruled Mide and Tethba. Much about him is legend, but the dynasties he founded dominated Ireland for centuries.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The early Irish annals say little about Niall, and his dates rest on later reckoning: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-inisfallen">Annals of Inisfallen</a> place his death before 382 and the Chronicon Scotorum in 411, while the Annals of the Four Masters date his reign to 379–405 and Geoffrey Keating to 368–395. Because his sons are recorded between 429 and 516, Kathleen Hughes and Francis J. Byrne argue that events were pushed back to suit an early date for Saint Patrick; Byrne, following James Carney, dates his death to about 452. The High Kingship itself became a reality only in the 9th century.</p><p>The tale of the sons of Eochaid Mugmedón, possibly of the 11th century, makes Niall the son of the High King Eochaid by his second wife, Cairenn Chasdub, daughter of a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>; Keating calls her a daughter of the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Left on the ground at birth for fear of Eochaid's first wife Mongfind and raised by the poet Torna, Niall later returned to Tara and freed his mother from forced labour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In the legend a druid, Sithchenn, tested Eochaid's five sons in a burning forge, from which Niall saved an anvil, and then sent them hunting; only Niall kissed the hag guarding a well, who became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">Sovereignty</a> of Ireland and promised the kingship to his descendants. Another story has Mongfind poison her brother Crimthann, leaving the high kingship to Niall. T. F. O'Rahilly held that Niall and his sons broke up the ancient kingdom of Ulster, and he and Byrne argue that the late sources preserve genuine traditions of raids on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>All sources name Eochaid, son of Énnae Cennsalach of Leinster, as his killer and place his death outside Ireland: on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a>, in Brittany, among Pictish bards in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> or beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>. Byrne suggests he died raiding Roman Britain. His body is said to lie at Faughan Hill near Navan in County Meath, and his nephew Nath Í succeeded him. His epithet is explained by hostages from the five provinces and from Scotland or other peoples, or, as O'Rahilly suggests, by the nine hostages owed by the Airgíalla. A 2006 genetic claim that millions of men descend from him is no longer thought plausible.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Niall of the Nine Hostages” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ailech emerged in the early medieval period as the kingdom of the northern Uí Néill, descendants of <b>Niall of the Nine Hostages</b> who expanded into the north-west in the 5th and 6th centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ailech">Kingdom of Ailech</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rulers in the early medieval period came from Cairpre and Maine, sons of <b>Niall of the Nine Hostages</b>, making it part of the southern Ui Neill world centred on Mide.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tethba">Kingdom of Tethba</a></li><li><blockquote>Tir Chonaill, the land of Conall, was named after Conall Gulban, a son of <b>Niall of the Nine Hostages</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tir-chonaill">Kingdom of Tir Chonaill</a></li><li><blockquote>Tir Eoghain, 'Eogan's land', descends from the Cenel nEogain, the northern Ui Neill kindred traced to Eogan, son of <b>Niall of the Nine Hostages</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tir-eoghain">Kingdom of Tir Eoghain</a></li></ul></section>
